title: Anonymous member enums collide to Module::UnnamedEnum (Nadeo DB data loss)
updated_at: "2026-08-29T15:23:46Z"
---
Found while implementing B014 (GH #59 typedb pipeline), 2026-08-24. NOT a
regression — pre-existing since the Nadeo-DB loader was written.

## Symptom
Inline/anonymous member enums in `OpenplanetNext.json` have no distinct name, so
the loader keys them all as `<Module>::UnnamedEnum`. 66 inline member-enum type
names collide within Nadeo modules — e.g. 15 distinct anonymous enums all key to
`Control::UnnamedEnum`. Whichever one lands first via `or_insert` wins; the other
14 are silently dropped from the index.

## Already fixed (partially) on branch feat/typedb-pipeline
`add_nadeo` now registers modules and types in sorted name order
(`src/typedb/index.rs`), so a given DB always yields the SAME index — the
determinism half is fixed and guarded by
`loading_the_same_db_twice_yields_identical_enums`. Before that fix, two loads of
identical bytes produced different indexes (HashMap iteration order), which made
`typedb diff` report 8 phantom changed enums when diffing the fixture against
itself.

## Still open: the data loss
Only one of each collision group is queryable. Consequences: enum values from the
dropped enums are unknown to the checker (missed diagnostics, and possibly wrong
`No matching symbol` / unknown-member behaviour if any rule ever treats the
Nadeo enum surface as authoritative), and completion misses them.

## Why it was NOT fixed on that branch (decision)
Splitting distinct anonymous enums onto separate keys changes the name-resolution
surface and therefore risks moving the FP baselines (tm-editor-plus-plus 2 errors
/ 1428 warnings, tm-dips-plus-plus 1 error / 258 warnings). That is its own
red/green + dogfood exercise, not a rider on a pipeline branch.

## Work
- Key inline member enums by their declaring context (e.g. `Module::Class::Field`
  or a synthesized `Module::Class_FieldEnum`) instead of `Module::UnnamedEnum`.
- Decide and document what the checker should accept for such an enum's values
  (they are reachable in-game via the member's type).
- Regenerate/verify counts: 66 colliding names, `Control` worst at 15.
- Gate: FP fixture error counts unchanged; run the tm-* fixtures and explain any
  warning-count delta.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: load the fixture Next DB and assert every distinct inline member enum is
independently queryable (assert count == 66 distinct keys, not 1-per-module);
assert a specific known collision group (Control's 15) all resolve. Variants:
enum with duplicate value names across two collided enums; a collided enum whose
values are referenced from a fixture plugin; determinism guard still green.

## Provenance
Discovered by the B014 lane's own plan-mandated test
(`diff_repo_fixture_against_itself_is_empty` reporting `(0, 0, 8)`), which is
exactly the tripwire GH #59 was asked to build.

## Decision (coordinator, 2026-08-29) — key scheme

Key inline/anonymous member enums by their declaring context rather than
<Module>::UnnamedEnum. Preferred key: <Module>::<Class>::<Field> when the
enum is a member's inline type; fall back to a stable synthesized suffix if
the declaring member cannot be identified. The key MUST be deterministic
(sorted-order registration is already in place) and stable across loads of
identical bytes — the existing
loading_the_same_db_twice_yields_identical_enums guard stays green.

Checker acceptance: values of such an enum are reachable in-game through the
member's type, so the checker should accept them wherever the member's type is
in play. Do not tighten any rule as part of this task — the goal is to stop
DROPPING enums, not to start rejecting more code.

Gate: FP fixture ERROR counts must be unchanged (tm-editor-plus-plus 2,
tm-dips-plus-plus 1, tm-archivist 2, tm-counter 4, tm-dashboard 4). Warning
deltas are permitted but every delta must be explained in the commit message.

Independent lane — no file overlap with B017/B019/B029.

